1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,521
Here are two possible resting mixtures.

2
00:00:02,521 --> 00:00:06,851
The amounts of B and C are different, but A
is exactly one in both.

3
00:00:06,851 --> 00:00:11,893
Can reactions make both mixtures stable,
while keeping that same resting value

4
00:00:11,893 --> 00:00:12,216
of A?

5
00:00:12,733 --> 00:00:17,179
Yes. Three chemicals suffice. But the
reactions need three independent

6
00:00:17,179 --> 00:00:21,942
directions of change, and at least six
different combinations of molecules.

7
00:00:21,942 --> 00:00:26,197
To see why, first let us turn one reaction
into a changing picture.

8
00:00:26,733 --> 00:00:31,490
In this teaching example, one A meets one B
and becomes two Bs.

9
00:00:31,490 --> 00:00:36,700
Count before and after. A decreases by one,
while B increases by one.

10
00:00:36,700 --> 00:00:40,701
The arrow tells us the change caused by one
reaction.

11
00:00:41,233 --> 00:00:46,205
Mass action says that the reaction rate is
proportional to the concentrations

12
00:00:46,205 --> 00:00:51,241
of the ingredients. With rate constant two,
concentration three of A, and four

13
00:00:51,241 --> 00:00:54,985
of B, the rate is two times three times
four: twenty-four.

14
00:00:55,533 --> 00:01:00,510
So this reaction contributes minus
twenty-four to A's rate of change, and plus

15
00:01:00,510 --> 00:01:04,976
twenty-four to B's. Adding contributions
from every reaction gives the

16
00:01:04,976 --> 00:01:09,825
complete equations. A resting state means
every net rate is zero, even while

17
00:01:09,825 --> 00:01:11,037
reactions continue.

18
00:01:11,567 --> 00:01:17,044
Now use the actual network. C can produce A
while remaining present.

19
00:01:17,044 --> 00:01:22,763
A together with C can remove A. Both
reaction constants are one, so the

20
00:01:22,763 --> 00:01:25,583
opposing rates are c and a times c.

21
00:01:26,100 --> 00:01:31,811
Subtract the loss from the gain. A's rate
is c times one minus a.

22
00:01:31,811 --> 00:01:35,237
Below one, A rises. Above one, A falls.

23
00:01:35,237 --> 00:01:40,860
At a positive resting state, c is positive,
so A must equal one.

24
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:45,915
This is absolute concentration robustness,
or A C R: the same concentration at

25
00:01:45,915 --> 00:01:50,257
every positive steady state. It does not
say that A is fixed during motion.

26
00:01:50,257 --> 00:01:53,904
Nor does it say that every starting mixture
eventually settles.

27
00:01:54,433 --> 00:01:59,160
Complete the network with these six
combinations and eight directed reactions.

28
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:01,220
A combination is called a complex.

29
00:02:01,220 --> 00:02:04,613
Both sides of every arrow contain at most
two molecules.

30
00:02:04,613 --> 00:02:07,945
That restriction on both sides matters for
the theorem.

31
00:02:08,467 --> 00:02:13,281
A stable resting state pulls sufficiently
small disturbances back towards it.

32
00:02:13,281 --> 00:02:16,407
Two different stable resting states make a
switch.

33
00:02:16,407 --> 00:02:21,096
Merely finding two resting states is
weaker: one could repel a disturbance,

34
00:02:21,096 --> 00:02:23,035
like a ball balanced on a hill.

35
00:02:23,566 --> 00:02:27,557
Let us find every positive resting state of
our network.

36
00:02:27,557 --> 00:02:29,981
We already know that a equals one.

37
00:02:29,981 --> 00:02:33,188
C stops changing when six a equals b times
c.

38
00:02:33,188 --> 00:02:37,678
Substitute one, then divide by positive b:
c equals six over b.

39
00:02:38,199 --> 00:02:43,260
B's equation still has to vanish.
Substitute c equals six over b, and

40
00:02:43,260 --> 00:02:43,847
multiply

41
00:02:43,847 --> 00:02:49,422
by b. The resulting expression factors into
minus b minus two, times b minus

42
00:02:49,422 --> 00:02:51,255
three, times b minus six.

43
00:02:51,799 --> 00:02:54,920
A product is zero when one factor is zero.

44
00:02:54,920 --> 00:02:59,824
So b is two, three, or six. Divide six by
each value to recover c.

45
00:02:59,824 --> 00:03:05,470
We get exactly the three positive resting
mixtures shown here, with A always

46
00:03:05,470 --> 00:03:05,767
one.

47
00:03:06,299 --> 00:03:11,328
This curve shows B's rate only after
imposing a equals one and c equals six

48
00:03:11,328 --> 00:03:14,948
over b. Its three crossings locate the
resting states.

49
00:03:14,948 --> 00:03:19,239
It is a restricted algebraic picture, not
the motion of the full

50
00:03:19,239 --> 00:03:20,915
three-dimensional system.

51
00:03:21,433 --> 00:03:26,511
Separate exact stability checks show that
the two outer mixtures attract small

52
00:03:26,511 --> 00:03:30,743
disturbances, while the middle mixture has
a direction of escape.

53
00:03:30,743 --> 00:03:34,258
These two simulated paths illustrate the
two outcomes.

54
00:03:34,258 --> 00:03:38,946
The stability claim comes from exact
calculations, not from these sample

55
00:03:38,946 --> 00:03:39,337
paths.

56
00:03:39,866 --> 00:03:42,306
Why do three independent directions matter?

57
00:03:42,306 --> 00:03:45,257
Imagine three amounts with their total
fixed at two.

58
00:03:45,257 --> 00:03:47,697
Increasing one forces a decrease elsewhere.

59
00:03:47,697 --> 00:03:51,045
You can choose two amounts freely; the
third is determined.

60
00:03:51,045 --> 00:03:53,882
The motion is confined to a two-dimensional
sheet.

61
00:03:54,399 --> 00:03:57,760
A known three-chemical network makes this
concrete.

62
00:03:57,760 --> 00:04:01,385
These reactions conserve the total number
of molecules.

63
00:04:01,385 --> 00:04:05,734
At total concentration two, there are two
positive resting states.

64
00:04:05,734 --> 00:04:09,951
C equals one half in both, but only one of
the states is stable.

65
00:04:10,499 --> 00:04:15,363
So counting chemicals is different from
counting independent directions.

66
00:04:15,363 --> 00:04:19,753
This earlier network has three chemicals
but only two directions.

67
00:04:19,753 --> 00:04:25,021
Our six-complex network has three of each,
with no conserved total restricting

68
00:04:25,021 --> 00:04:26,507
its motion to a sheet.

69
00:04:27,033 --> 00:04:31,646
The main theorem says that, in two
dimensions, two distinct nondegenerate

70
00:04:31,646 --> 00:04:35,691
positive resting states in the same class
cannot both be stable.

71
00:04:35,691 --> 00:04:40,494
Nondegenerate means that the local response
has no zero direction within the

72
00:04:40,494 --> 00:04:44,097
sheet. At least one state has a real
expanding direction.

73
00:04:44,633 --> 00:04:47,703
Here is the geometric structure of the
argument.

74
00:04:47,703 --> 00:04:50,069
Fix one sheet of compatible mixtures.

75
00:04:50,069 --> 00:04:54,994
Robustness places every resting state on
the line where one concentration has

76
00:04:54,994 --> 00:04:58,001
its special value. We now look along that
line.

77
00:04:58,533 --> 00:05:03,216
Because the reaction rates contain at most
products of two concentrations,

78
00:05:03,216 --> 00:05:07,835
they are quadratic at worst. A
factorisation lemma reduces resting states

79
00:05:07,835 --> 00:05:07,962
on

80
00:05:07,962 --> 00:05:10,177
the line to roots of one quadratic.

81
00:05:10,177 --> 00:05:14,037
Two distinct nondegenerate roots are
therefore the only ones.

82
00:05:14,566 --> 00:05:16,984
Watch the slopes at the two crossings.

83
00:05:16,984 --> 00:05:19,529
One points upwards, the other downwards.

84
00:05:19,529 --> 00:05:24,364
Their signs are opposite. The proof
connects those slopes to local stability

85
00:05:24,364 --> 00:05:28,564
through another factor, called ell, which
varies linearly with the

86
00:05:28,564 --> 00:05:29,518
concentrations.

87
00:05:30,066 --> 00:05:34,396
For two directions, the determinant
describes the product of the local

88
00:05:34,396 --> 00:05:38,911
stretching factors. A negative determinant
forces one contracting and one

89
00:05:38,911 --> 00:05:43,674
expanding direction: a saddle. The proof's
determinant equals minus ell times

90
00:05:43,674 --> 00:05:45,282
the slope at the crossing.

91
00:05:45,799 --> 00:05:51,136
If ell keeps the same sign at both resting
states, the opposite slopes force

92
00:05:51,136 --> 00:05:56,192
opposite determinant signs. One determinant
is negative, so one state is

93
00:05:56,192 --> 00:06:01,248
unstable. The only remaining possibility is
that ell itself changes sign

94
00:06:01,248 --> 00:06:02,863
between the two states.

95
00:06:03,399 --> 00:06:06,402
A linear function changes sign by crossing
zero.

96
00:06:06,402 --> 00:06:08,592
Draw its zero line through the gap.

97
00:06:08,592 --> 00:06:13,221
In the remaining proof cases, robustness
constrains the rate of the robust

98
00:06:13,221 --> 00:06:17,788
chemical along this line: it cannot vanish
there away from the prescribed

99
00:06:17,788 --> 00:06:18,663
concentration.

100
00:06:19,199 --> 00:06:22,324
Now the chemistry supplies the crucial
restriction.

101
00:06:22,324 --> 00:06:25,816
A chemical cannot be consumed when none of
it is present.

102
00:06:25,816 --> 00:06:30,043
And a reaction starting with two copies
cannot end with more than two

103
00:06:30,043 --> 00:06:33,719
molecules. Together these facts limit how
its rate can bend.

104
00:06:34,266 --> 00:06:37,976
Hold the other concentrations fixed and
increase this one.

105
00:06:37,976 --> 00:06:42,966
Its rate is a line or a downward-bending
quadratic, starting at zero or above.

106
00:06:42,966 --> 00:06:47,443
Once the rate becomes negative, it stays
negative as the concentration

107
00:06:47,443 --> 00:06:50,130
increases. Watch that forced continuation.

108
00:06:50,666 --> 00:06:55,707
In one representative sign case, the
proposed sign change requires a positive

109
00:06:55,707 --> 00:07:00,683
rate farther to the right. But we already
have a negative rate at the nearer

110
00:07:00,683 --> 00:07:05,397
point. The bending rule forces a negative
rate at the farther point too.

111
00:07:05,397 --> 00:07:06,314
Contradiction.

112
00:07:06,833 --> 00:07:10,779
The written proof checks the other sign and
geometric cases as well.

113
00:07:10,779 --> 00:07:15,190
They either give this kind of contradiction
or expose an expanding direction

114
00:07:15,190 --> 00:07:19,601
directly. The conclusion is universal
within the assumptions: two dimensions

115
00:07:19,601 --> 00:07:21,401
cannot support a robust switch.

116
00:07:21,933 --> 00:07:26,369
Our actual network supplies the matching
upper example in dimension three.

117
00:07:26,369 --> 00:07:30,985
A separate counting argument, using the
deficiency one theorem, excludes five

118
00:07:30,985 --> 00:07:34,882
complexes. Since our network has six, the
minimum is exactly six.

119
00:07:34,882 --> 00:07:36,981
The counting proof is in the paper.

120
00:07:37,499 --> 00:07:40,455
The number of chemicals has a simpler
story.

121
00:07:40,455 --> 00:07:45,494
You can take a two-chemical switch and add
an independent chemical produced

122
00:07:45,494 --> 00:07:50,465
and removed at equal rates. That third
chemical has a fixed resting value.

123
00:07:50,465 --> 00:07:54,563
So three species already suffice by a
decoupled construction.

124
00:07:55,099 --> 00:07:59,377
That simple construction limits the novelty
of the species result.

125
00:07:59,377 --> 00:08:03,978
The stronger content is the two-dimensional
obstruction and the coupled

126
00:08:03,978 --> 00:08:08,709
witnesses. In our running example,
reactions involving A also involve the

127
00:08:08,709 --> 00:08:13,699
other chemicals; the switch and robust
component are not separate subsystems.

128
00:08:14,233 --> 00:08:19,810
The earlier work of Kaihnsa, Nguyen and
Shiu asked whether stability increases

129
00:08:19,810 --> 00:08:23,886
all the minimum sizes. Dimension rises from
two to three.

130
00:08:23,886 --> 00:08:27,819
Complexes rise from five to six. Species
stay at three.

131
00:08:27,819 --> 00:08:31,681
The minimum number of reactant complexes
remains open.

132
00:08:32,199 --> 00:08:36,754
How are the examples checked? Two
independent programs reconstruct the

133
00:08:36,754 --> 00:08:41,764
reaction equations and use exact arithmetic
for resting states and stability.

134
00:08:41,764 --> 00:08:46,774
These are algebraic certificates, rather
than a conclusion drawn from rounded

135
00:08:46,774 --> 00:08:49,767
graphs or a handful of numerical
trajectories.

136
00:08:50,299 --> 00:08:54,859
Lean checks the two coupled examples'
vector fields, their exact positive

137
00:08:54,859 --> 00:08:58,981
resting states, and robustness. It does not
check stability or the

138
00:08:58,981 --> 00:09:03,353
minimum-size theorems. Those claims rely on
the written proofs and the

139
00:09:03,353 --> 00:09:05,851
separately described exact calculations.

140
00:09:06,399 --> 00:09:11,761
Keep three distinctions. Robustness
describes positive resting states, not all

141
00:09:11,761 --> 00:09:16,298
times. Multiple resting states need not be
multiple stable states.

142
00:09:16,298 --> 00:09:21,591
And species, complexes, and independent
directions measure different kinds of

143
00:09:21,591 --> 00:09:24,615
size. Our example separates all three
ideas.

144
00:09:25,133 --> 00:09:29,898
The mathematical correctness is supported
with high confidence; novelty is

145
00:09:29,898 --> 00:09:32,732
more cautiously bounded by the prior
search.

146
00:09:32,732 --> 00:09:37,434
These are mathematical networks, not
experimentally demonstrated devices.

147
00:09:37,434 --> 00:09:40,911
The paper and editable materials accompany
this video.

148
00:09:40,911 --> 00:09:43,037
Narration uses a synthetic voice.
